---
import "../styles/paper.css";
import "katex/dist/katex.min.css";
import katex from "katex";

const k = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true });
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Two players claim cells of an n by n grid; the first to own four corners of a square wins. The first player wins exactly when n is at least 5, by a machine-checked strategy. Play against it."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="masthead">
      <h1>The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards</h1>
      <p class="byline">Sean Reid · August 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="abstract">
      <strong>Abstract.</strong> Two players alternately claim cells of an
      <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid; the first to hold all four corners
      of an axis-aligned square wins. We show the first player has a winning strategy
      precisely when <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />, and that all smaller
      boards are drawn under optimal play. The strategy is explicit, and the
      searches that verify it are formalized in Lean 4, so each verified board
      size comes with a machine-checked proof.
    </p>

    <figure class="game">
      <sag-board n="5"></sag-board>
      <figcaption>
        Figure 1. Play the game: the bot moves first, replaying the strategy
        below move for move, and you reply. Change n to explore; below 5 × 5
        neither side can force a win.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The game</h2>
    <p>
      Played on the cells of an <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid, P1 moving
      first. A winning set is any
      <span
        set:html={k("\\{(r,c),\\,(r,c{+}s),\\,(r{+}s,c),\\,(r{+}s,c{+}s)\\}")}
      /> with <span set:html={k("s \\ge 1")} />: the four corners of an
      axis-aligned square, from 2 × 2 blocks up. A full board with no completed
      square is a draw. Tilted squares do not count. Both players are after the
      same squares, so even a player who cannot win anymore can still block, and
      on the boards below 5 × 5 blocking is enough: neither player can force a
      win.
    </p>

    <figure class="diagram">
      <svg
        viewBox="-4 -4 152 152"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A 6 by 6 grid showing the corners of a 2 by 2 block and of a square of side 3"
      >
        <defs>
          <pattern
            id="grid6"
            width="24"
            height="24"
            patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
          >
            <path
              d="M24 0H0V24"
              fill="none"
              stroke="var(--rule)"
              stroke-width="0.8"
              opacity="0.5"></path>
          </pattern>
        </defs>
        <rect width="144" height="144" fill="url(#grid6)"></rect>
        <rect
          width="144"
          height="144"
          fill="none"
          stroke="var(--rule)"
          stroke-width="0.8"
          opacity="0.5"></rect>
        <rect
          x="12"
          y="108"
          width="24"
          height="24"
          fill="none"
          stroke="var(--accent)"
          stroke-width="1.5"
          stroke-dasharray="4 3"></rect>
        <rect
          x="60"
          y="12"
          width="72"
          height="72"
          fill="none"
          stroke="var(--accent)"
          stroke-width="1.5"
          stroke-dasharray="4 3"></rect>
        <circle cx="12" cy="108" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="36" cy="108" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="12" cy="132" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="36" cy="132" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="60" cy="12" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="132" cy="12" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="60" cy="84" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="132" cy="84" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        Figure 2. Winning shapes: the corners of a 2 × 2 block and of a square
        of side 3. Only the corners must be held.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="theorem">
      <span class="label">Theorem 1.</span> The first player has a winning strategy
      in the square achievement game on the
      <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board if and only if
      <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />.
    </div>

    <h2>History</h2>
    <p>
      Harary called games like this achievement games: both players build toward
      the same target, and the first to finish wins. Erickson asked what happens
      for the axis-aligned square on an
      <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid, and the question is recorded on the
      Open Problem Garden. Two ends of the answer were known. Jenrich reported computer
      searches giving draws on 3 × 3 and 4 × 4 and a first-player win on 5 × 5. At
      the other end, Bacher and Eliahou proved that every two-coloring of a 15 × 15
      grid contains a monochromatic square, so no game on a board of size 15 or more
      can end in a draw; a standard strategy-stealing argument then gives the first
      player a win, though it produces no strategy. In between, from 6 × 6 through
      14 × 14, the outcome was open. We close that range, and the proof is a strategy
      you can watch: the bot in Figure 1 is playing it.
    </p>

    <h2>The strategy</h2>
    <p>
      On every turn the strategy checks two reflexes before anything else:
      complete one of your own squares if you can; otherwise, if the opponent is
      one cell from completing theirs, block it. What remains is choosing the
      quiet moves, and those come from a fixed lookup table. The strategy opens
      at (2,2), answers the opponent's first reply with a tabled second move,
      and the first two replies together select a plan: a short list of cells
      played in order.
    </p>
    <p>
      Plans are forcing. Each plan move puts three corners of some square in
      place, so the opponent must block at once or lose, until one move creates
      two threats and no single block answers both. The opponent's forced blocks
      pile up too, and sometimes complete three corners of their own square; the
      block reflex answers those counter-threats, and the search that generated
      the table only accepted plans that keep the initiative through such
      exchanges.
    </p>
    <p>
      One table serves every board from 6 × 6 up, for two reasons. First, its
      plans never leave the 6 × 6 corner region of the board, so every square
      the strategy builds exists on every board containing that region. Second,
      a reply far from the corner region can join a square through two of the
      strategy's stones only from the region's own rows and columns, so far
      replies are looked up by geometric class rather than by exact position.
      The 5 × 5 board is the odd one out: it is too cramped for pure forcing,
      and exhaustive analysis shows that no all-forcing table wins there. Its
      table comes instead from a full solution of the 5 × 5 game and includes
      quiet moves that fork on the opponent's reply.
    </p>

    <h2>Why believe a computer search</h2>
    <p>
      For each board size, an exhaustive search plays the strategy against every
      reply sequence; at 9 × 9 that is over three million positions. This game
      has its own cautionary tale: the first published search for 5 × 5
      contained an error and was later corrected. The search here is implemented
      three times independently, in Python, in Lean, and in the TypeScript bot
      on this page, and all three agree on every size tested. Only one of the
      three carries mathematical weight: the Lean checker comes with a
      machine-checked soundness theorem: if the checker returns true, a winning
      strategy tree exists for the abstract game. The strategy tables enter the
      development as plain data, so nothing about how they were found needs to
      be trusted.
    </p>

    <h2>Toward every board at once</h2>
    <p>
      For boards of size 15 and up the win is indirect: no draw is possible, and
      the second player cannot have a winning strategy, so the first player
      does. That argument names no move. We conjecture that the table strategy
      wins on every board of size 6 and up, which would replace the indirection
      with one explicit strategy for all of them. A patient opponent, though,
      can drop stones far from the corner region, aligned so that the squares
      through them exist only on much larger boards, and harvest forced
      exchanges from them. The reflexes answer each threat, but every answer
      costs a tempo, and a table tuned on one finite board can be led off script
      on a larger one. We have not ruled that out for every board at once yet;
      the sizes through 14 × 14 are settled unconditionally.
    </p>
    <figure class="diagram">
      <svg
        viewBox="-8 -24 356 352"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A 16 by 16 board with a farm square through the corner region and three far opponent stones"
      >
        <defs>
          <pattern
            id="grid16"
            width="20"
            height="20"
            patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
          >
            <path
              d="M20 0H0V20"
              fill="none"
              stroke="var(--rule)"
              stroke-width="0.7"
              opacity="0.45"></path>
          </pattern>
        </defs>
        <rect width="320" height="320" fill="url(#grid16)"></rect>
        <rect
          width="320"
          height="320"
          fill="none"
          stroke="var(--rule)"
          stroke-width="0.7"
          opacity="0.45"></rect>
        <rect width="120" height="120" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.09"
        ></rect>
        <rect
          x="70"
          y="10"
          width="240"
          height="240"
          fill="none"
          stroke="var(--accent)"
          stroke-width="1.5"
          stroke-dasharray="5 4"></rect>
        <circle
          cx="70"
          cy="10"
          r="6"
          fill="var(--paper)"
          stroke="var(--ink)"
          stroke-width="1.5"></circle>
        <circle
          cx="310"
          cy="10"
          r="6"
          fill="var(--paper)"
          stroke="var(--ink)"
          stroke-width="1.5"></circle>
        <circle
          cx="70"
          cy="250"
          r="6"
          fill="var(--paper)"
          stroke="var(--ink)"
          stroke-width="1.5"></circle>
        <circle
          cx="310"
          cy="250"
          r="8"
          fill="none"
          stroke="var(--ink)"
          stroke-width="1.5"
          stroke-dasharray="4 3"></circle>
        <text
          x="70"
          y="-8"
          text-anchor="middle"
          font-size="14"
          fill="var(--ink)"
          font-style="italic">t</text
        >
        <text
          x="310"
          y="-8"
          text-anchor="middle"
          font-size="14"
          fill="var(--ink)"
          font-style="italic">o1</text
        >
        <text
          x="52"
          y="255"
          text-anchor="end"
          font-size="14"
          fill="var(--ink)"
          font-style="italic">o2</text
        >
        <text
          x="328"
          y="255"
          text-anchor="start"
          font-size="14"
          fill="var(--ink)"
          font-style="italic">z</text
        >
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        Figure 3. A farm on the 16 × 16 board. The opponent's far replies o1 and
        o2 align with the corner region (shaded); once a forced block lands on
        t, the dashed square threatens at the dashed cell z. The square does not
        fit any smaller board.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Formalization and downloads</h2>
    <p>
      The proof is formalized in Lean 4: the game semantics, an executable
      verifier with a machine-checked soundness theorem, and the strategy tables
      as certificates; the assembly for arbitrary board size is in progress.
      Both artifacts are built from this repository on every deploy:
      <a href="downloads/square-achievement-paper.pdf">the paper (PDF)</a> and
      <a href="downloads/square-achievement-formalization.tar.gz"
        >the Lean development (tar.gz)</a
      >.
    </p>

    <footer>
      Code MIT · text CC BY 4.0 · built from the square-achievement repository.
    </footer>

    <script>
      import "../game/board";
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
